From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yevhen Salitrynskyi <ysalitrynskyi@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 21:08:16 -0400
Subject: teach the tab strip that a tab can be in another Space

TabStripModel gains IsTabHidden(index), meaning "the user cannot currently see
this tab", and an embedder-settable predicate behind it. IsTabCollapsed keeps
its old meaning; the five places that were really asking the broader question --
SelectRelativeTab's is_tab_invalid, the three preferences for what to activate
after a close, and GetNextExpandedActiveTab -- now ask IsTabHidden instead.

The predicate is a callback rather than a call into the Spaces code, so
TabStripModel gains no dependency and there is no cycle between tabs and spaces.
Nothing sets it by default, so with no Spaces in play every one of those call
sites behaves exactly as before.

SpaceModel now owns the tab-to-Space map, keyed by TabHandle so a closed tab
cannot be confused with a later one at the same address, and installs the
predicate on its window's tab strip. A tab the model has never seen counts as
visible: a window whose tabs predate Spaces must not blink out of existence.

Consequence, already true and worth stating: Ctrl+Tab, and the choice of which
tab to activate when the active one closes, will skip tabs in other Spaces
without any further work, because they route through the predicate.

Tabs are still all shown -- the views do not consult this yet.

Why: this is the join between Spaces and the tab strip, and doing it as one
predicate keeps it to five call sites in a file that takes 153 commits a year.
Removable when: the tab collection tree grows a first-class notion of tab sets
(ADR 0015), at which point hiding becomes a property of the tree.
